{"reference":"Jeremiah 37","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":37,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Within the Book of Jeremiah","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jeremiah 37’s siege, threatened burning of the city, false accusation, imprisonment, and diminishing bread belong to a wider portrayal of catastrophe and survival across the book. The received book brings together poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, repeated accounts, restoration material, oracles concerning nations, and an appendix; its long compositional and textual history remains debated. This perspective helps readers see the chapter as part of a larger, varied book that repeatedly depicts life under disaster, while not requiring every part to be read in the same literary mode.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_037_94fd41f9e3c6a220c681aa17"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}